-- Q4566: Tests of Controls [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
-- STEM: During the audit of Thornhill Industries, the engagement team plans to rely on the client''s three-way matching control over cash disbursements to red
-- CHOICES (word counts):
--   A ★ (13w) Obtain evidence about whether controls operated effectively throughout the period of intended relian
--   B   (9w) Ensure management is complying with all laws and regulations
--   C   (5w) Determine the appropriate audit fee
--   D   (8w) Evaluate the competence of the entity's accounting staff
-- CURRENT EXPLANATION: Tests of controls are designed to evaluate whether controls operated effectively throughout the period for which the auditor intends to rely on them. 
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330, tests of controls evaluate whether controls operated effectively throughout the period of intended reliance. Operating effectiveness means the control was applied consistently and as designed by personnel with appropriate authority and competence. This evidence supports the auditor''s planned reduction in substantive testing.
Wrong (B): Compliance with laws and regulations is assessed through other procedures, not through tests of controls over financial reporting.
Wrong (C): Determining the audit fee is an administrative matter unrelated to control testing.
Wrong (D): Evaluating accounting staff competence is part of understanding the entity, not the purpose of testing specific controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4566;

-- Q4570: Tests of Controls [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
-- STEM: An auditor plans to rely on controls that were tested in the prior year and have not changed. Under auditing standards, the auditor:
-- CHOICES (word counts):
--   A   (14w) May rely on the prior year's control testing without performing any current year tests
--   B ★ (19w) Must retest the controls every year but may reduce the extent of testing if the controls have not ch
--   C   (15w) Must perform the same extent of testing as in the prior year regardless of changes
--   D   (14w) May rely on the prior year's testing for up to five years before retesting
-- CURRENT EXPLANATION: Auditing standards require the auditor to test controls in each audit period when the auditor plans to rely on them. However, the auditor may adopt a 
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, the auditor must test controls in each audit period when planning to rely on them. However, a rotation approach may be used where unchanged controls are tested at least once every three audits with reduced extent of testing in intervening years. Some testing must always occur in the current period.
Wrong (A): The auditor cannot simply rely on prior-year testing alone without performing any current-year procedures.
Wrong (C): The extent of testing may be reduced if controls have not changed; identical testing each year is not required.
Wrong (D): There is no five-year reliance period; standards require testing at least once every three audits for unchanged controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4570;

-- Q4571: Tests of Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
-- STEM: During the audit of Sterling Medical Devices, the engagement team plans to rely on the client''s invoice approval controls and must test their operati
-- CHOICES (word counts):
--   A   (4w) Inquiry of entity personnel
--   B   (4w) Observation of control operation
--   C ★ (4w) Confirmation of accounts receivable
--   D   (5w) Inspection of documents and reports
-- CURRENT EXPLANATION: Confirmation of accounts receivable is a substantive procedure, not a test of controls. Methods for testing controls include inquiry, observation, ins
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Confirmation of accounts receivable is a substantive procedure used to verify account balances, not a test of controls. Under AU-C 330, methods for testing controls include inquiry, observation, inspection of documents showing evidence of control performance, and reperformance of the control by the auditor.
Wrong (A): Inquiry of entity personnel is a recognized method for testing controls, though it must be combined with other procedures.
Wrong (B): Observation of control operation is a valid test of controls that provides evidence of how the control functions in practice.
Wrong (D): Inspection of documents and reports showing evidence of control performance is a standard test of controls method.$EXPL$
