-- Issues: #3
-- STEM: An auditor compares the current year's gross margin percentage to the prior year and to industry averages. This is an example of:
-- CHOICES (word counts):
--   A   (4w) A test of controls
--   B   (5w) A substantive test of details
--   C ★ (3w) An analytical procedure
--   D   (4w) An audit sampling technique
-- CURRENT EXPLANATION: Comparing financial ratios (such as gross margin percentage) to prior years and industry averages is a common analytical procedure. Analytical procedu
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Comparing financial ratios such as gross margin percentage to prior years and industry averages is a classic analytical procedure. Under AU-C 520, analytical procedures involve evaluating financial information through analysis of plausible relationships among financial and non-financial data, including comparisons of recorded amounts to auditor-developed expectations.
Wrong (A): A test of controls evaluates whether internal controls operated effectively, not financial ratio comparisons.
Wrong (B): A substantive test of details involves examining individual transactions or balances, not ratio analysis.
Wrong (D): Audit sampling involves selecting specific items for testing, not comparing aggregate ratios.$EXPL$
