-- Q4597: Analytical Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
-- STEM: An auditor notices during planning analytics that the current year's days sales outstanding (DSO) has increased significantly compared to prior years 
-- CHOICES (word counts):
--   A   (7w) The entity is collecting receivables more quickly
--   B   (6w) The entity's inventory management has improved
--   C ★ (12w) Potential issues with accounts receivable collectibility, overstatement, or changes in credit terms
--   D   (8w) The entity's cost of goods sold has decreased
-- CURRENT EXPLANATION: An increase in DSO (days sales outstanding) indicates that it is taking longer to collect receivables. This may signal potential issues with collectib
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): An increase in DSO indicates that receivables are taking longer to collect. Under AU-C 520, this may signal collectibility problems requiring an increased allowance, potential overstatement from fictitious sales or unrecorded returns, or changes in credit terms. The auditor should investigate the underlying cause during risk assessment.
Wrong (A): Higher DSO means slower collections, not faster; this choice describes the opposite of what the ratio indicates.
Wrong (B): DSO measures receivables collection efficiency, not inventory management; inventory turnover would address inventory performance.
Wrong (D): Cost of goods sold is unrelated to DSO; a decrease in COGS would affect gross margin ratios, not receivables metrics.$EXPL$
